UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 28, Lesson 1: Composing with rhythm and melody', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Composing with rhythm and melody starts with a clear beat, a rhythm bed of one or two bars, then a melody shape that fits. Draft in syllables or simple notation, then test by clapping and singing. Edit for too many leaps if pitching is hard.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCreate a two-bar rhythm and describe a four-note melody contour that fits it.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness.\n\nWATCH FOR\nWriting pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCompose and notate a four-bar piece with rhythm bed + melody. Perform it and note one edit you made.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Composing with rhythm and melody starts with a clear beat, a rhythm bed of one or two bars, then a melody shape that fits. Draft in syllables or simple notation, then test by clapping and singing. Edit for too many leaps if pitching is hard.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-MUS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Compose and notate a four-bar piece with rhythm bed + melody. Perform it and note one edit you made. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness. Reasoning: I used compose by attending to rhythm bed rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning. Correction: Strong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness. Why: the correction restores accurate use of compose.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating compose on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with compose, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness. This matches the lesson skill for compose.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness.","Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Create a two-bar rhythm and describe a four-note melody contour that fits it. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Strong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness.","Careful use of rhythm bed with clear evidence.","Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning.","Composing with rhythm and melody starts with a clear beat, a rhythm bed of one or two bars, then a melody shape that fits. Draft in syllables or simple…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising compose?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named rhythm bed, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the rhythm bed used."],"question":"A learner is practising compose. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness. uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Strong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness.","Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about compose?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about compose.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about rhythm bed?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: Rhythm | ta ti-ti ta ta | Melody contour up-up-down-home on C D E C. Draft revised to reduce a leap.","Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Composing with rhythm and melody starts with a clear beat, a rhythm bed of one or two bars, then a melody… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning.","Compose and notate a four-bar piece with rhythm bed + melody. Perform it and note one edit you made."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of compose?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating compose on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness. Reasoning: I used compose by attending to rhythm bed rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with compose, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Writing pitches with no rhythm. Both layers need planning. Correction: Strong answer: Rhythm written with ta/ti-ti; contour named with step/leap awareness. Why: the correction restores accurate use of compose.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w28-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise compose.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the 